UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 37, Lesson 4: Fluency: reading graphs', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Graph-reading fluency practises keys, scales, comparisons and simple totals from displays.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nKey: 1 symbol = 5. Three symbols show how many?\n\nFEEDBACK\n15.\n\nWATCH FOR\nSkipping the scale on column graphs misreads heights.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nComplete an eighteen-item graph fluency set. Circle the key/scale before answering each time.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Graph-reading fluency practises keys, scales, comparisons and simple totals from displays.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Fluency and review","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-DATA-02"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Complete an eighteen-item graph fluency set. Circle the key/scale before answering each time. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: 15. Reasoning: I used fluency by attending to graph rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Skipping the scale on column graphs misreads heights. Correction: 15. Why: the correction restores accurate use of fluency.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Skipping the scale on column graphs misreads heights. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses fluency with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with fluency, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values. / Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"15. This matches the lesson skill for fluency. The correct choice applies the lesson skill; the others use a common mistake or ignore the evidence.","options":["Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","15.","Skipping the scale on column graphs misreads heights.","Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only."],"question":"Key: 1 symbol = 5. Three symbols show how many?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Skipping the scale on column graphs misreads heights. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["15.","Careful use of graph with clear evidence.","Skipping the scale on column graphs misreads heights.","Graph-reading fluency practises keys, scales, comparisons and simple totals from displays."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising fluency?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named graph, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the graph used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing zero as if it does not change the value."],"question":"A learner is practising fluency. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value or quantity.","options":["Skipping the scale on column graphs misreads heights.","Show the places, values or steps and say why each step is valid.","Write only a final number with no working.","Change digits to look neater without checking place value."],"question":"Which working note best supports a mathematics answer about fluency?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the method; the others break it.","options":["Skipping the scale on column graphs misreads heights.","Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","Set: read a column; use a picture key; find the difference between two categories. Prefer key-first habits."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Graph-reading fluency practises keys, scales, comparisons and simple totals from displays. — which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus.","options":["Pick the longer-looking working automatically.","Delete units so the answers appear to match.","Average the two answers without checking either.","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed."],"question":"Which error should you repair first if two methods disagree?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same method.","options":["Solve a fresh problem with different numbers and explain the method.","Retype the worked model numbers as the answer.","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work."],"question":"Which practice choice shows transfer of fluency?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Useful feedback names the failed step and shows corrected working.","options":["Praise neat handwriting even when the places are wrong.","Tell the learner to skip working and only keep the final digits.","Just add more digits until it looks longer.","Name the exact place or step that failed, then show corrected working for fluency."],"question":"Which feedback comment is most useful after a mistake?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses fluency with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: 15. Reasoning: I used fluency by attending to graph rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with fluency, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Skipping the scale on column graphs misreads heights. Correction: 15. Why: the correction restores accurate use of fluency.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y4-w37-l4","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise fluency.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Statistics and Probability","success_criteria":["I can use fluency with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify graph and explain why it matters.","I can practise read independently on a new prompt.","I can notice scale needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Graph-reading fluency practises keys, scales, comparisons and simple totals from displays. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Graph-reading fluency practises keys, scales, comparisons and simple totals from displays."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about fluency?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSet: read a column; use a picture key; find the difference between two categories. Prefer key-first habits.\n\nTRY FIRST\nKey: 1 symbol = 5. Three symbols show how many?\n\nFEEDBACK\n15.","explain":"Graph-reading fluency practises keys, scales, comparisons and simple totals from displays.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Skipping the scale on column graphs misreads heights. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Graph fluency.","caption":"Scale before height read.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Fluency</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">key; scale; compare</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Habit</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">circle key first</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Set: read a column; use a picture key; find the difference between two categories. Prefer key-first habits. Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Set: read a column; use a picture key; find the difference between two categories. Prefer key-first habits. Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about fluency?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSet: read a column; use a picture key; find the difference between two categories. Prefer key-first habits. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Key use vs skipped scale.","caption":"Eighteen items.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">3×5 key</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">15</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">skip scale</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about fluency?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSkipping the scale on column graphs misreads heights. Here is a more productive model: Set: read a column; use a picture key; find the difference between two categories. Prefer key-first habits.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\n15.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Skipping the scale on column graphs misreads heights. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about fluency?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nGraph-reading fluency practises keys, scales, comparisons and simple totals from displays. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 37, Lesson 4: Fluency: reading graphs","unit_number":10,"unit_title":"Data, chance and Stage 2 review","week":37,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nSet: read a column; use a picture key; find the difference between two categories. Prefer key-first habits.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","wor